-   "notes": "THREE BUILDING FACTS ABOUT CHICAGO IN ONE PARAGRAPH, THREE WEEKS BEFORE THE SCENE DATE.\n\n1. THE SAUGANASH HAS JUST BEEN REFITTED — 'repaired, fitted up and furnished with neatness and taste'. The building is old news; its CONDITION on 1 July 1835 is not, and this says it was freshly done up and not shabby. 'so far as it goes' is the editor's qualification and it means small, not poor.\n\n2. THE LAKE HOUSE IS UNDER CONSTRUCTION IN KINZIE'S ADDITION AND IS NOT YET FINISHED OR NAMED. 'There is a new house building in Kinzie's addition [to be] named we understand, the \"Lake House.\"' Both halves matter: it is a BUILDING SITE at the scene date, north of the main branch, and even its name is reported at second hand ('we understand'). Anything the reconstruction puts on that spot on 1 July 1835 should be a structure going up, not a hotel open for business. This is the only dated statement of the Lake House's state that this corpus has produced.\n\n3. NO HOUSE IN CHICAGO IS BIG ENOUGH, and the editor says where the big one should be: 'about double the size of that latter, erected this side of the river about the centre of Lake Street or in that vicinity.' That is a PROPOSAL and not a building — nothing stood there — but it fixes the Lake House's scale by comparison (a house double it would be the town's largest) and it tells you where the commercial centre of gravity was felt to be: the middle of Lake Street, south of the river.\n\nWITH THE OTHER CLAIMS IN THIS READING RANGE the town's public houses at the scene date are: the Sauganash (refitted, unplaced here), the Exchange Coffee House (corner of Lake and Franklin), the Eagle Coffee House or Trowbridge's (Dearborn Street vicinity), the Tremont House (Dearborn Street), the New-York House (Lake Street), and the Lake House (building, Kinzie's Addition). Six houses and not one of them, on the editor's account, adequate.\n\nThe column is clipped at the right edge line by line; every supply is bracketed and none of them invents a name, a size or a street."
